    "product_id": "507419d0-d37c-47c9-b226-d8892fda823a",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 191016\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n316 AM MST Sat Sep 19 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Dry and tranquil weather conditions with near zero rain chances \n  can be expected through Monday.\n\n- Near to slightly above normal temperatures will persist through \n  next week.\n\n- A low pressure system and moisture return early next week will \n  bring a return of rain chances to at least the high terrain of \n  northern and eastern Arizona. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nUpper-level water vapor satellite imagery early this morning shows a \nlow pressure system in the northern Great Basin and a high pressure \nsystem centered over eastern Texas. In between these two features \nthere is dry southwesterly flow over the Southwest. With dry \nsouthwesterly flow still in place over our region moisture continues \nto decrease. PWATs have decreased another 0.1-0.3\" from yesterday\nafternoon with PWATs currently ranging from around 0.4\" across \nthe western deserts increasing to around 0.8\" across the higher \nterrain areas in southern Gila County. Dry southwesterly flow will\ncontinue across the region today before going more westerly \ntomorrow as the aforementioned low pressure system moves eastward\ninto the northern Plains. As the drying continues PWATs are \nexpected to bottom out late tonight around 0.3-0.6\". We will \nfinally see this drier air make its way to the surface today as \ndew points fall into the 30s and 40s by late this afternoon/early \nthis evening. There will be some moisture return from the Gulf of \nCalifornia into far SW AZ and SE CA tomorrow with dew points going\nback into the 50s for them, otherwise dew points will generally \nbe in the 40s for most other locations. Due to all the dry air \nacross our region this weekend, there will be mostly clear skies \nwith zero chances for showers or storms. Additionally H5 heights \nwill remain largely unchanged (generally around 588 dm), so \ntemperatures will also remain fairly steady through the weekend. \nAfternoon highs will be in the upper 90s to low 100s (upper 80s to\nmid 90s) with morning lows in the 70s to around 80 degrees (mid \n60s to low 70s) across the lower deserts (higher terrain). This \nwill result in widespread Minor HeatRisk across the CWA. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nModels are in good agreement that a Pacific low will move to the \nsouth-central coast of California by early Monday and then move \neastward onshore throughout the day. If it weren't for the lack of \nmoisture on Monday it would be a decent set up for a transitional \nsevere weather event as there will be potential for some \nfrontogenesis with upper-level jet support and higher shear. \nHowever, while PWATs will increase on Monday, they look to remain \naround or slightly below 1\" (0.6-1.0\"). \n\nThen on Tuesday the low will slowly weaken as it moves NE into NV/UT \nbefore getting swept up into the large scale flow on Wednesday as it \nmoves into the northern Plains. At the same time a weak ridge will \nbe in place over TX and will start to strengthen on Wednesday. This \nwill result in southwesterly flow over our area. With the moisture \nreturn and some upper-level forcing from the aforementioned low, \nrain chances will return to the higher terrain areas in northern \nand eastern Arizona. Weak large scale troughing will remain \nacross the eastern Pacific/West Coast through the end of next week\nand will be battling the ridge of high pressure over TX. By the \nend of the workweek the ridge will try to retrograde into NM/AZ. \nAdditionally ensemble members bring a surge of moisture into our \narea from the SE pushing PWATs back into a 1.0-1.3\" range. As a \nresult NBM/WPC PoPs for the end of next week have 30-50% chance \nof showers and storms across the higher terrain in southern Gila \nCounty and around 10-20% across the lower deserts of south-central\nAZ.\n\nTemperatures next week will remain largely unchanged and be in the \nupper 90s to low 100s across the lower deserts and in the upper 80s \nto mid 90s across the higher terrain. Morning lows will generally be \nin the 70s to to low 80s across the lower deserts and in the 60s \nacross the higher terrain areas. Morning lows do look to start to \ncreep up by the end of next week (more morning lows in the low 80s \nacross the lower deserts). As a result widespread Minor HeatRisk \nwill continue through the beginning of next week with Moderate \nHeatRisk returning to the lower deserts for the latter half of next \nweek as the morning lows creep up and daily normals decrease.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1015Z. \n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nNo aviation weather concerns expected through Sunday morning under\nclear skies. Winds will continue to follow typical diurnal\ntendencies with speeds around 8 kts or less. There will likely be\na period of light southerly cross-runway winds at KPHX for most of\nthe afternoon ahead of prevailing W-SW winds by late\nafternoon/evening. Extended periods of calm and vrb conditions \ncan also be anticipated during the overnight and morning periods.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo aviation weather concerns expected through Saturday night under\nmostly clear skies. Winds will follow seasonal diurnal patterns at\nboth terminals with mainly a W component at KIPL and S-SW at KBLH.\nWnd speeds should remain around 8 kts or less with limited gusts,\nand extended periods calm conditions to light vrb can be expected.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nOverall there are no fire weather concerns through the next week.\nConditions will continue to dry out today with no chances for rain\ncontinuing through Monday. Slight chances for showers and storms\nreturn to the higher terrain areas of southern Gila County\nbeginning on Tuesday. MinRHs will be around 10-25% today and\ntomorrow before increasing to around 15-30% for the first half of\nnext week. Overnight recoveries will range from fair to good\nacross the region through the middle of next week. Besides a \npotential increase in breeziness early next week with a weather \nsystem, winds will mostly follow typical diurnal patterns with \nmodest afternoon upslope/upvalley gustiness (15-25 mph).\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Berislavich\nLONG TERM...Berislavich\nAVIATION...Benedict \nFIRE WEATHER...Berislavich/Benedict\n",
